  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nConvective trends are the main concern for the rest of the day,\nwith an MCV sliding out of the Manitoba, North Dakota, and \nMinnesota area to the east-southeast. It should move into the \nNorthland this afternoon, bringing an initial round of showers \nand storms to the forecast area. The current convection is \nelevated in nature, tapping into the extreme amount of MUCAPE we\nsaw on this mornings KINL upper air sounding. What this \nconvection does over the next few hours is still somewhat \nuncertain. If it remains elevated and simply drifts to the \nsoutheast, we may have mainly a large hail threat. However, the \nairmass over our region is destabilizing, and we have 40 to 50 \nknots of 0-6km shear. If we can break the cap and the storms can\nbecome surface based, we could be dealing with an expanding \nQLCS and associated damaging wind threat. We are in wait and see\nmode for now. Additional storm development later this afternoon\nand tonight is going to depend on flow moving up over the 850mb\nbaroclinic zone, and initiation will be difficult to forecast \nvery far ahead of time. Apart from the storms, dangerous heat \npersists this afternoon, with temperatures already well into the\n80s and Brainerd has risen above 90 already. The heat \nheadlines, both warning and advisory, continue as is with hazy, \nsmoky skies. \n\nFor Monday, temperatures temper slightly as winds shift to the \nnorthwest and bring in some drier air, but conditions will \nremain unseasonably warm. Heat indices will still approach the \nlow 90s, especially in the Brainerd Lakes area. Isolated severe \nthunderstorms remain possible in the early morning as an upper \nlevel shortwave and cold front traverse the region. Any \norganized storms could produce large hail and damaging winds, \nmainly early in the day. Areas of wildfire smoke will also \npersist, keeping skies fairly hazy across the Northland.\n\nHigh pressure builds into the region for Tuesday and Wednesday,\nbringing drier conditions and at least a few days off from the \noppressive humidity. While daytime highs will remain above \nnormal in the 80s to low 90s, the drier airmass will make it \nfeel more comfortable across the region. Generally quiet, dry, \nand pleasant weather is expected to hold firm for both days with\ncomfortable overnight lows in the upper 50s and 60s. Isolated, \ndiurnally driven showers and storms may be possible during the \nafternoon and early evening. \n\nHeading into Thursday and Friday, the quiet stretch comes to an\nend as the next shortwave trough and associated cold front \napproaches from the west. This system will bring our next \nwidespread chance for showers and thunderstorms to the area. \nHigh temperatures will continue to reach the 80s, with humidity \nlevels creeping back up slightly as southerly flow returns ahead\nof the advancing system. \n\nAs we move into next weekend, near to slightly above normal \ntemperatures will persist. Afternoon highs will generally remain\nin the 80s. We will be in a somewhat stagnant pattern with the \nupper level ridge sliding east to over the Great Plains, keeping\nthe storm track generally to our north. We may get occasional \nwaves of showers and storms which dive across the area, but \nnothing consistent is appearing in the model runs just yet.",
